(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_ecosystem=[{id:"neet-2026-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecosystem productivity",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. Productivity \u2014 I. Gross primary productivity minus respiration losses
B. Net primary productivity \u2014 II. Rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers
C. Gross primary productivity \u2014 III. Rate of biomass production
D. Secondary productivity \u2014 IV. Rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],c:1,s:`Productivity is the rate of biomass production.
Net primary productivity is gross primary productivity (GPP) minus respiration losses (R).
Gross primary productivity is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis.
Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.
So A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2026-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition process terms",q:`Match List I with List II:
List-I \u2014 List-II
A. Decomposition \u2014 I. Accumulation of dark coloured amorphous colloidal substance
B. Detritus \u2014 II. Release of inorganic nutrients by the activity of microbes in soil
C. Mineralisation \u2014 III. Breaking down of complex organic matter into inorganic substances.
D. Humification \u2014 IV. Dead remains of plants and animals including fecal matter`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:2,s:`Decomposition is breaking down of complex organic matter into inorganic substances.
Detritus is the dead remains of plants and animals including fecal matter; it is the raw material for decomposition.
Degradation of humus by microbes that releases inorganic nutrients is called mineralisation.
Accumulation of the dark coloured amorphous substance called humus is humification.`},{id:"neet-2026-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"Ecological pyramids represent the relationship between the organisms at different trophic levels and they are generally inverted for:",o:["Pyramid of energy in pond ecosystem","Pyramid of biomass in sea","Pyramid of number in grassland","Pyramid of biomass in grassland"],c:1,s:`Pyramid of number in grassland ecosystem is upright.
Pyramid of energy in pond ecosystem is upright.
Pyramid of biomass in grassland is upright.
Pyramid of biomass in sea is inverted.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q161",pn:161,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Food chain, trophic levels",q:"Which of the following are primary consumers in a food chain?",o:["Predators","Herbivores","Carnivores","Parasites"],c:1,s:"Primary consumers in a food chain are the herbivores."},{id:"neet-2026r-q178",pn:178,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Energy flow",q:`Which of the following statements are Correct?
(a) Energy flow from producers to consumers is unidirectional
(b) Energy pyramid can never be inverted
(c) Transfer of energy follows the 1% law
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a) and (b) only","(a) and (c) only","(b) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (c)"],c:0,s:`Energy flow is unidirectional: plants capture solar energy, then food passes from producers to decomposers.
The pyramid of energy is always upright and can never be inverted, because some energy is lost as heat at each step.
Energy transfer follows the 10 per cent law: only 10 per cent of the energy passes to the next trophic level.
So (c), the 1% law, is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2025-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Productivity and energy flow",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : The primary source of energy in an ecosystem is solar energy.
Statement II : The rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis in an ecosystem is called net primary productivity (NPP).
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:2,s:`The primary source of energy in an ecosystem is solar energy.
The rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis is gross primary productivity, not net primary productivity.
Hence statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2025-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecosystem productivity",q:"Which of the following is the unit of productivity of an Ecosystem?",o:["gm\u207B\xB2","KCal m\u207B\xB2","KCal m\u207B\xB3","(KCal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`The rate of biomass production is called productivity.
It is expressed in gm\u207B\xB2yr\u207B\xB9 or (KCal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9 to compare the productivity of different ecosystems.`},{id:"neet-2025-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Energy flow",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: In ecosystem, there is unidirectional flow of energy of sun from producers to consumers.
Statement II: Ecosystems are exempted from 2nd law of thermodynamics.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:2,s:`The sun is the only source of energy for all ecosystems on Earth, except the deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem.
Energy flow is unidirectional from the sun to producers and then to consumers.
Ecosystems are not exempted from the second law of thermodynamics.
They need a constant supply of energy to make the molecules they need, to counter the universal tendency towards increasing disorderliness.`},{id:"neet-2024-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Productivity",q:`In an ecosystem if the Net Primary Productivity (NPP) of first trophic level is:
100x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9 what would be the GPP (Gross Primary Productivity) of the third trophic level of the same ecosystem?`,o:["10 x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9","(100x)/(3x) (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9","(x)/(10) (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9","x(kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`The source gives no solution for this question.
Using the 10% law: the second trophic level gets about 10x and the third about x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9.
So the printed answer is x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here. The printed answer is [4].`},{id:"neet-2023-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition and detritus food chain",q:`Identify the correct statements :
A. Detrivores perform fragmentation
B. The humus if further degraded by some microbes during mineralization
C. Water soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil and get precipitated by a process called leaching
D. The detritus food chain begins with living organisms
E. Earthworms break down detritus into smaller particles by a process called catabolism.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["D, E, A only","A, B, C only","B, C, D only","C, D, E only"],c:1,s:`The detritus food chain begins with detritus, which is dead organic matter, not living organisms. So D is wrong.
Breaking detritus into smaller particles is fragmentation, done by earthworms. Catabolism is done by saprotrophic bacteria and fungi, which turn detritus into simple inorganic substances. So E is wrong.
A, B and C are correct.`},{id:"neet-2023-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary productivity",q:`In the equation
GPP \u2013 R = NPP
GPP is Gross Primary Productivity
NPP is Net Primary Productivity
R here is ____________.`,o:["Reproductive allocation","Photosynthetically active radiation","Respiratory quotient","Respiratory loss"],c:3,s:`Plants use a large part of GPP in respiration.
GPP minus the respiratory losses (R) gives NPP.
So R is the respiratory loss.`},{id:"neet-2023-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ozone depletion units",q:"The thickness of ozone in a column of air in the atmosphere is measured in terms of :",o:["Kilobase","Dobson units","Decibels","Decameter"],c:1,s:`The thickness of ozone in a column of air from ground to top of the atmosphere is measured in Dobson units (DU).
Noise is measured in decibels.
Note: Environmental issues has no chapter in the list; Ecosystem used as nearest.`},{id:"neet-2023-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Water pollution and eutrophication",q:"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?",o:["The amount of some toxic substances of industrial waste water increases in the organisms at successive trophic levels","The micro-organisms involved in biodegradation of organic matter in a sewage polluted water body consume a lot of oxygen causing the death of aquatic organisms","Algal blooms caused by excess of organic matter in water improve water quality and promote fisheries","Water hyacinth grows abundantly in eutrophic water bodies and leads to an imbalance in the ecosystem dynamics of the water body"],c:2,s:`Algal blooms give the water a colour, spoil water quality and cause fish deaths.
So the statement that they improve quality and promote fisheries is wrong.
Note: Environmental issues has no chapter in the list; Ecosystem used as nearest.`},{id:"neet-2023-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Water pollution and biomagnification",q:"Which of the following statements is correct?",o:["Algal Bloom decreases fish mortality","Eutrophication refers to increase in domestic sewage and waste water in lakes.","Biomagnification refers to increase in concentration of the toxicant at successive trophic levels.","Presence of large amount of nutrients in water restricts 'Algal Bloom'."],c:2,s:`Biomagnification is the rise in concentration of a toxicant at each successive trophic level.
Lots of nutrients in water promote algal bloom, and algal bloom increases fish mortality.
Eutrophication is the natural ageing of a lake by nutrient enrichment.`},{id:"neet-2023-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Air pollution control",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Electrostatic precipitator is most widely used in thermal power plant
Statement II : Electrostatic precipitator in thermal power plant removes ionising radiations
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."],c:3,s:`Electrostatic precipitators are the most widely used device in thermal power plants.
They remove over 99 percent of the particulate matter in the exhaust, not ionising radiation.
So Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q108",pn:108,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological succession climax",q:"All successions irrespective of the habitat proceed to which type of climax community?",o:["Edaphic","Xeric","Mesic","Hydrophytic"],c:2,s:"Whatever the starting habitat, succession moves towards a mesic climax community."},{id:"neet-2022-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Air pollution control devices",q:"The device which can remove particulate matter present in the exhaust from a thermal power plant is :",o:["Incinerator","Electrostatic Precipitator","Catalytic Convertor","STP"],c:1,s:`An electrostatic precipitator removes over 99% of particulate matter from power plant exhaust.
Catalytic converters are for vehicles.
STPs treat sewage.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q122",pn:122,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Hydrarch succession",q:"The pioneer species in a hydrarch succession are :",o:["Filamentous algae","Free-floating angiosperms","Submerged rooted plants","Phytoplanktons"],c:3,s:`Hydrarch succession starts in water, for example a newly formed pond.
The pioneers are phytoplanktons: tiny autotrophs such as diatoms and unicellular, colonial or filamentous green and blue-green algae.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q125",pn:125,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Pioneer species",q:"The species that come to appear in bare area are called",o:["Species of seral community","Pioneer species","Invasive species","Competitive species"],c:1,s:`The first species to colonise a bare area are pioneer species.
Examples are lichens on rock and phytoplankton in ponds.`},{id:"neet-2022-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Decomposition is a process in which the detritus is degraded into simpler substances by microbes.
Statement II: Decomposition is faster if the detritus is rich in lignin and chitin.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`Decomposers break complex organic matter into simple inorganic substances, so Statement I is correct.
The rate depends on the chemical make-up of the detritus and on climate.
Detritus rich in lignin and chitin decomposes slowly.
Detritus rich in nitrogen and water-soluble sugars decomposes faster.
So Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q140",pn:140,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Carbon cycle figures",q:`Match List-I with List-II :
List-I: (a) Carbon dissolved (b) Annual fixation of Carbon through Photosynthesis (c) PAR captured by Plants (d) Productivity of oceans
List-II: (i) 55 billion tons (ii) 71% (iii) 4 \xD7 10\xB3 kg (iv) 2 to 10%
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`71% of carbon is dissolved in the oceans, which are the largest carbon reservoir.
Annual carbon fixation by photosynthesis is about 4 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 kg.
Only 2 to 10% of the PAR (about half of incident light) is captured by plants.
Ocean productivity is 55 billion tons, low because of scarce nitrogen and nutrients.
Note: The paper prints '4 \xD7 10\xB3 kg' in List-II (iii); its solution gives 4 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 kg. The printed question text is kept.`},{id:"neet-2022-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Phosphorus cycle",q:"Which one of the following will accelerate phosphorus cycle?",o:["Volcanic activity","Weathering of rocks","Rain fall and storms","Burning of fossil fuels"],c:1,s:`The phosphorus cycle is a sedimentary cycle.
Its reservoir is the earth's crust (lithosphere).
Weathering of rocks releases phosphorus and speeds up the cycle.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q160",pn:160,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary productivity",q:"The amount of biomass or organic matter produced per unit area over a time period by plants during photosynthesis is called:",o:["Net primary production","Secondary production","Primary production","Gross primary production"],c:3,s:"Biomass made per unit area in a given time by plants in photosynthesis is gross primary production."},{id:"neet-2022-q181",pn:181,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition",q:"Detritivores breakdown detritus into smaller particles. This process is called:",o:["Fragmentation","Humification","Decomposition","Catabolism"],c:0,s:`Detritivores such as earthworms break detritus into smaller pieces.
This step is called fragmentation.`},{id:"neet-2021-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"Which of the following statements is not correct ?",o:["Pyramid of numbers in a grassland ecosystem is upright.","Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted.","Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally upright.","Pyramid of energy is always upright."],c:2,s:`In the sea the producers are tiny phytoplankton that multiply fast but have a short life, so their standing biomass is small.
Zooplankton and other consumers live longer, so their biomass is larger.
So the sea biomass pyramid is usually inverted, and statement 3 (upright) is wrong.
A grassland number pyramid is upright, and an energy pyramid is always upright, so those statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2021-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary productivity",q:"In the equation GPP \u2013 R = NPP ; R represents :",o:["Respiration losses","Radiant energy","Retardation factor","Environmental factor"],c:0,s:`GPP is gross primary productivity, the total food made.
Plants use some of it in respiration (R).
What remains is NPP, so R stands for respiration losses.`},{id:"neet-2021-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Standing state",q:"The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus and calcium present in the soil at any given time, is referred as :",o:["Standing crop","Climax","Climax community","Standing state"],c:3,s:`The amount of inorganic nutrients in the soil at a given time is the standing state.
Standing crop is the living material at each trophic level, and climax community is the final stable stage of succession.`},{id:"neet-2020-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Trophic levels",q:`Match the trophic levels with their correct species examples in grassland ecosystem.
(a) Fourth trophic level  (i) Crow
(b) Second trophic level  (ii) Vulture
(c) First trophic level  (iii) Rabbit
(d) Third trophic level  (iv) Grass
Select the correct option :`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Grass is the first trophic level (producer).
Rabbit eats grass: second level.
Crow feeds on the herbivore-eaters: third level.
Vulture is at the fourth level.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`},{id:"neet-2020-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Productivity",q:"In relation to Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity of an ecosystem, which one of the following statements is correct ?",o:["Gross primary productivity is always more than net primary productivity.","Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity are one and same.","There is no relationship between Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity.","Gross primary productivity is always less than net primary productivity."],c:0,s:`NPP = GPP - respiratory losses.
So GPP is always more than NPP.`},{id:"neet-2019-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"Which of the following ecological pyramids is generally inverted?",o:["Pyramid of biomass in a forest","Pyramid of biomass in a sea","Pyramid of numbers in grassland","Pyramid of energy"],c:1,s:`In the sea, producers are tiny phytoplankton with a small standing biomass.
The consumers (fishes) have a larger biomass.
So the biomass pyramid in a sea is inverted.
The energy pyramid is always upright.`},{id:"neet-2019-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Nitrogen cycle",q:"Thiobacillus is a group of bacteria helpful in carrying out :",o:["Nitrification","Denitrification","Nitrogen fixation","Chemoautotrophic fixation"],c:1,s:`Some bacteria, such as Thiobacillus, change nitrate back to free nitrogen.
This process is denitrification.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2019-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Greenhouse effect",q:"Which of the following pairs of gases is mainly responsible for green house effect ?",o:["Nitrogen and Sulphur dioxide","Carbon dioxide and Methane","Ozone and Ammonia","Oxygen and Nitrogen"],c:1,s:`The main greenhouse gases are carbon dioxide and methane.
Nitrogen and oxygen do not trap heat.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2018-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"What type of ecological pyramid would be obtained with the following data ? Secondary consumer : 120 g, Primary consumer : 60 g, Primary producer : 10 g",o:["Inverted pyramid of biomass","Pyramid of energy","Upright pyramid of numbers","Upright pyramid of biomass"],c:0,s:`The data are in grams, so they are biomass.
Producers have the smallest biomass (10 g) and the top consumers the largest (120 g), so the pyramid is inverted.
Such pyramids occur in aquatic ecosystems.`,sfig:{w:220,h:108,alt:"Inverted pyramid of biomass: 120 g at the top, 60 g in the middle, 10 g at the bottom.",d:[["r",55,10,112,28,""],["r",75,38,72,28,""],["r",93,66,36,30,""],["t",111,29,"120 g","m"],["t",111,57,"60 g","m"],["t",111,86,"10 g","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2017-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Stratification",q:"Presence of plants arranged into well defined vertical layers depending on their height can be seen best in :",o:["Temperate Forest","Tropical Savannah","Tropical Rain Forest","Grassland"],c:2,s:`Tropical rain forests have many tall trees, shrubs, herbs and climbers.
They show clear vertical stratification, more than the other options.`},{id:"neet-2017-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Biomass",q:"Which ecosystem has the maximum biomass ?",o:["Lake ecosystem","Forest ecosystem","Grassland ecosystem","Pond ecosystem"],c:1,s:"Forests have large trees, so they hold the highest biomass of the listed ecosystems."},{id:"neet-2016-q087",pn:87,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Sewage and BOD",q:"A river with an inflow of domestic sewage rich in organic waste may result in -",o:["Increased population of aquatic food web organisms","An increased production of fish due to biodegradable nutrients","Death of fish due to lack of oxygen","Drying of the river very soon due to algal bloom"],c:2,s:`Organic waste is broken down by microbes that use dissolved oxygen.
BOD rises and dissolved oxygen falls.
Fish die from lack of oxygen.`},{id:"neet-2016-q089",pn:89,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Agricultural practices",q:"A system of rotating crops with legume or grass pasture to improve soil structure and fertility is called -",o:["Contour farming","Strip farming","Shifting agriculture","Ley farming"],c:3,s:`Ley farming alternates crops with legume or grass pasture.
The legume fixes nitrogen and the pasture improves soil structure and fertility.
Note: Agriculture chapter is not in the NEET list; placed in Ecosystem as the nearest.`},{id:"neet-2016-q101",pn:101,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ozone depletion",q:"Depletion of which gas in the atmosphere can lead to an increased incidence of skin cancers -",o:["Ozone","Ammonia","Methane","Nitrous oxide"],c:0,s:`The ozone layer in the stratosphere absorbs harmful UV rays.
When ozone is depleted, more UV reaches the earth and damages skin cells, which can cause skin cancer.`},{id:"neet-2016-q102",pn:102,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Cropland ecosystem",q:"Which one of the following is a characteristic feature of cropland ecosystem ?",o:["Least genetic diversity","Absence of weeds","Ecological succession","Absence of soil organisms"],c:0,s:`A cropland grows one or a few uniform crop varieties, so it has the least genetic diversity.
Weeds and soil organisms are present, and succession is prevented by farming.`},{id:"neet-2016-q109",pn:109,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary succession",q:"Which of the following would appear as the pioneer organisms on bare rocks?",o:["Liverworts","Mosses","Green algae","Lichens"],c:3,s:`On bare rock, lichens come first.
They secrete acids that weather the rock and form a little soil, so mosses can follow.`},{id:"neet-2016-q115",pn:115,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Terminology",q:"The term ecosystem was coined by :",o:["A.G. Tansley","E. Haeckel","E. Warming","E.P. Odum"],c:0,s:"The word 'ecosystem' was coined by A.G. Tansley (1935)."},{id:"neet-2016b-q131",pn:131,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Producers in unusual ecosystems",q:"The primary producers of the deep-sea hydrothermal vent ecosystem are",o:["green algae","chemosynthetic bacteria","blue-green algae","coral reefs"],c:1,s:`No sunlight reaches the deep sea, so photosynthetic organisms cannot live there.
Chemosynthetic bacteria make food using energy from oxidising chemicals such as hydrogen sulphide from the vents.
They are the primary producers.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q176",pn:176,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Water pollution and BOD",q:"Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) may not be a good index for pollution for water bodies receiving effluents from",o:["domestic sewage","dairy industry","petroleum industry","sugar industry"],c:2,s:`BOD measures the oxygen microbes use to break down organic matter in water.
Domestic sewage, dairy and sugar effluents are rich in biodegradable organic matter, so BOD reflects their pollution.
Petroleum industry effluents contain mostly chemicals that microbes cannot readily oxidise, so BOD is not a good index there.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q179",pn:179,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Eutrophication and oxygen",q:"A lake which is rich in organic waste may result in",o:["increased population of aquatic organisms due to minerals","drying of the lake due to algal bloom","increased population of fish due to lots of nutrients","mortality of fish due to lack of oxygen"],c:3,s:`Organic waste is broken down by decomposers, which use up the dissolved oxygen.
BOD rises and dissolved oxygen falls.
Fish and other aquatic animals then die from lack of oxygen.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q180",pn:180,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Biomagnification",q:"The highest DDT concentration in aquatic food chain shall occur in",o:["Phytoplankton","seagull","crab","eel"],c:1,s:`DDT is not broken down and builds up as it moves up the food chain (biomagnification).
The chain is phytoplankton \u2192 crab \u2192 eel \u2192 seagull.
The seagull is at the top, so its DDT concentration is highest.`}]})();
